{"ratings":[{"id":"C1","consequence":"secret","trigger":"unusual","reason":"A colored credential near the runner’s tail boundary can leave a secret fragment in persisted output and model feedback."},{"id":"C2","consequence":"secret","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A failing check that prints a formatted multiline credential can expose the complete credential in stored failure excerpts and feedback."},{"id":"C3","consequence":"secret","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Overlapping credentials split across output chunks can leave the sensitive extension of the longer credential visible."},{"id":"C4","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"normal","reason":"Any diagnostic containing the redaction marker is withheld in full, potentially depriving the operator of information needed to resolve a failure."},{"id":"C5","consequence":"nuisance","trigger":"normal","reason":"Ordinary API and event-stream objects can have terminal formatting removed and an unrelated metadata field added, with no established material failure."},{"id":"C6","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"unusual","reason":"A patch containing literal terminal escape bytes is served with different source text, which an operator could rely on when inspecting the change."},{"id":"C7","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A review prompt can omit literal escape sequences from a proposed source change, giving the reviewer inaccurate evidence and potentially causing a missed issue."},{"id":"C9","consequence":"compat","trigger":"unusual","reason":"After rollback, an older release can misclassify a saved successful result as a blocking confinement failure when its output contains a specially formatted error phrase."}]}